            <ArticleTitle>Subcutaneous targeted neuromodulation technique for the treatment of intractable chronic postthoracotomy pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>638-41</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jclinane.2009.10.018</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Subcutaneous targeted neuromodulation has been used successfully in chronic neuropathic pain. A 26 year-old patient with severe postthoracotomy pain and ipsilateral &quot;wing scapula&quot; due to intraoperative injury of the long thoracic nerve, is reported. Application of targeted neuromodulation resulted in immediate pain relief and marked improvement of shoulder function at one-year follow-up. The technique may be an effective alternative treatment of chronic and intractable postoperative painful conditions.</AbstractText>
